## Styling Rules

These rules define how we style UI across the project to keep CSS minimal, consistent, and maintainable.

### Core principles
- **Prefer `src/app/globals.css`**: Centralize shared styles and variables there. Add new global classes only when multiple places need the same style.
- **Do not duplicate existing classes**: Before adding a new class, search `globals.css`. If it already exists, reuse it; do not redefine or slightly tweak duplicates.
- **Avoid redundant defaults**: The `body` already sets base typography (normal font size, color, line-height). Headings (`h1`–`h6`) already define font sizes and weights. Do not restyle these unless a component has a specific, justified need.

### When adding styles
- **Component scope first**: If a style is unique to a component, keep it local (e.g., inline class names or component-level module). Only promote to `globals.css` if reused in 2+ places.
- **Minimal overrides**: Override only what differs from the default. Do not restate inherited properties (e.g., font-size for body text, heading weights/sizes).
- **Naming**: Use semantic, purpose-driven class names in `globals.css` (e.g., `btn-primary`, `card`, `container`), not presentational names like `blue-text`.

### Using utility classes from globals.css
- **Use utility classes as-is**: The `container` class is fully defined in `globals.css` (includes `w-full`, `mx-auto`, `px-4`, and max-width at xl breakpoint). Never add extra properties like `max-w-7xl`, `mx-auto`, or `px-4` when using `.container`.
- **Don't override utility classes**: Other utility classes (`.link-underline`, `.btn-solid`, `.btn-outline`, `.fs-lg`, `.fs-md`, `.fs-sm`, `.fs-xs`, `.property-swiper`, `.aspect-3/4`) are complete. Use them without adding extra CSS properties.
- **Example**:
  - Good: `<div className="container">...</div>` (uses full definition from globals.css)
  - Bad: `<div className="container mx-auto max-w-7xl">...</div>` (duplicates properties already in `.container`)
  - Bad: `<h2 className="h2 text-3xl">...</h2>` (duplicates font size already in `.h2`)

### Do / Avoid
- **Do**: Reuse classes defined in `globals.css` where applicable.
- **Do**: Add utilities/tokens (variables, color aliases, spacing scales) to `globals.css` when broadly useful.
- **Avoid**: Creating near-duplicate classes (e.g., `btn`, `btn-1`, `btn-primary-v2`). Refactor or extend thoughtfully.
- **Avoid**: Setting font sizes/weights on standard headings unless you are intentionally diverging from the global scale.
- **Avoid**: Applying base text styles to normal paragraphs; the `body` covers defaults.
- **Avoid**: Adding properties that are already included in utility classes (e.g., don't add `mx-auto` or `max-w-*` with `.container`).

### Checklist before adding a new class
1. Is this style used in multiple components? If no, keep it local to the component.
2. Does a suitable class already exist in `globals.css`? If yes, reuse it.
3. Are you restating defaults on `body` or headings? If yes, remove the redundant rules.
4. Is the class name semantic and reusable? If not, rename it.

### Examples
- Good: Use an existing `container` class from `globals.css` instead of redefining margins/padding.
- Good: Create `badge` in `globals.css` only after at least two components need the same badge styling.
- Bad: Define `h2 { font-weight: 700; }` when headings already set appropriate weights.
- Bad: Create `text-base` override on paragraphs; the `body` font size is already set globally.

### Updates to globals.css
- Prefer grouping related rules and documenting intent with short comments.
- Keep variables/tokens (colors, spacing) near the top for discoverability.
- When changing a global class, scan usages to avoid regressions.

By following these rules, we keep styles consistent, avoid duplication, and leverage `globals.css` effectively without unnecessary overrides.

### Colors, backgrounds, and variables
- **Always use CSS variables from `globals.css`**: All colors, backgrounds, borders, spacing, and other design tokens must reference variables defined in `globals.css`. Never use hardcoded values (e.g., `#fff`, `rgb()`, `#d0d0d0`, raw `px` values for spacing).
- **Borders**: Use border variables from `globals.css` (e.g., `--border-primary`, `--border-secondary`). Never use hardcoded border colors like `border-gray-200` or `border-[#d0d0d0]`.
- **Dark mode is handled in `globals.css`**: All variables (colors, backgrounds, borders, etc.) automatically switch for dark mode via CSS variable definitions. Never apply dark mode classes (`dark:`) or conditionally toggle styles in component code.
- **Typography from `globals.css`**: Use the predefined heading styles (`h1`-`h6` or `.h1`-`.h6` classes) and utility font size classes (`.fs-lg`, `.fs-md`, `.fs-sm`, `.fs-xs`) defined in `globals.css`. Do not override font sizes, weights, or line heights unless the design explicitly requires a deviation from the established scale.
- **Example**: Use `bg-(--bg-primary)`, `text-(--text-primary)`, `border-(--border-primary)` (Tailwind arbitrary value syntax with CSS variables) or directly reference `var(--bg-primary)`, `var(--text-primary)` in custom CSS. Never use `bg-white`, `text-black`, `border-gray-200`, or `dark:bg-gray-900`.

## Repository-wide AI Coding Rules

These are the exact rules the AI must follow when writing or editing code in this repository. They are derived from your development guide and tuned to this codebase.

### General
- Follow existing patterns first; consistency over novelty.
- Prefer composition over large components; keep pages thin and move logic to hooks.
- Never introduce a new pattern when an equivalent exists in the repo.

### Folder structure (per feature)
- Put new features under `src/features/{feature}/` with:
  - `api/`, `components/`, `constants/`, `hooks/`, `models/`, `pages/`, `validation/`, and `index.ts` in each folder.
- **Always use `constants/` folder**: For feature-specific constants, configuration data, static content, and mock data. Each constant file should have a clear purpose (e.g., `heroSection.ts`, `partners.ts`, `propertyDashboard.ts`).
- **Always use `models/` folder**: For TypeScript interfaces and types specific to the feature's domain (e.g., `property.ts`, `user.ts`). Export all types from `models/index.ts`.
- Always add local `index.ts` re-exports for cleaner imports from the feature folder.
- Co-locate component-specific helpers/types under the feature folder.

Note:
- This template is for future scale. Do not add folders that have no data/usage yet.
- If a feature currently only needs `components/` and `constants/`, keep only those. Add others when needed.
- **Always check existing feature folders** (like `src/features/home/`) to follow the established pattern before creating new structures.

### Component structure (Atomic Design)
- **Follow Atomic Design pattern**: All shared/reusable components must be organized under `src/components/` using atomic design principles:
  - **`atoms/`**: Basic building blocks (buttons, inputs, labels, icons). Self-contained, minimal props, no business logic.
  - **`molecules/`**: Simple combinations of atoms (dropdown with button and menu, search bar with input and icon, form field with label and input).
  - **`organisms/`**: Complex components made of molecules/atoms (AppHeader, AppFooter, navigation bars, complex forms).
- **Feature-specific components**: Components used only within a single feature stay in `src/features/{feature}/components/` (e.g., `HeroSection`, `PropertyDashboard`, `PropertyInsights`).
- **Export pattern**: Each level exports through `index.ts` for clean imports (e.g., `import { AppHeader, AppFooter } from '@/components/organisms'`).
- **Component placement decision**:
  - If used in 2+ features → move to `src/components/{atomic-level}/`
  - If feature-specific → keep in `src/features/{feature}/components/`
  - If unsure → start in feature folder, refactor when reused

### Naming
- Files: components `PascalCase.tsx`, hooks and utilities `camelCase.ts`, models `camelCase.ts`, constants `camelCase.ts`.
- Components: PascalCase and descriptive (e.g., `AddUserModal`, `UsersTable`).
- Interfaces/types: PascalCase without "I" prefix (e.g., `User`, `CreateUserRequest`).
- Constants files: Descriptive names matching their purpose (e.g., `heroSection.ts`, `partners.ts`, `propertyDashboard.ts`).
- One React component per file.

### TypeScript
- Strict types only; no `any`.
- Use `interface` for object shapes; `type` for unions/aliases.
- Use `import type { ... }` for type-only imports.

### Hooks (business logic)
- One public hook per feature (e.g., `hooks/useFeature.ts`) that encapsulates all business logic, state, side effects, and handlers.
- Use `useCallback` for event handlers; `useMemo` for computed values (filters, derived lists).
- Debounce text searches with `useDebounce(value, 500)`.
- Expose a single return object grouping data, state, and actions.

Note:
- The “one public hook per feature” is a recommended pattern. Implement it when the feature has real business logic; skip it for purely presentational features.

### Components
- Keep presentational; consume the feature hook for data/handlers.
- **Follow atomic design structure**: Place components in the appropriate atomic level (`atoms/`, `molecules/`, `organisms/`) based on complexity and composition.
- **Feature vs. shared components**: Feature-specific components stay in `src/features/{feature}/components/`. Only promote to `src/components/` when used across 2+ features.
- Tables: support search, pagination, loading/empty/error states.
- Do not perform data fetching directly in components; use feature hooks.

### Images and icons
- Use Next.js `Image` only; never `<img>`.
- Always specify width/height or `fill`; add meaningful `alt`.
- Use icons from phosphor-icons package.
- Never use inline `<svg>`. Create in assets and get from the `assets/index.ts` file.

### Performance
- `useMemo`: expensive transforms, dropdown options, filtered/sorted lists, API filters.
- `useCallback`: handlers passed to children or used in deps.
- `React.memo`: list items or components with expensive render.
- Lazy-load heavy components (charts, modals) with `lazy`/`Suspense`.

### Accessibility
- Use semantic elements; associate labels with inputs; ensure keyboard and focus behavior.
- Provide `aria-*` where appropriate.

### UI/styling
- Tailwind only; compose with `clsx` and `tailwind-merge`.
- Keep variants small and clear; avoid inline style duplication.

### Do and Don't
- Do
  - Follow feature folder template exactly (with `constants/`, `models/`, `components/`, etc.).
  - Follow atomic design pattern for all shared components (`atoms/`, `molecules/`, `organisms/`).
  - Use `constants/` folder for all static/configuration data within features.
  - Use `models/` folder for all TypeScript interfaces and types within features.
  - Keep page components thin.
  - Centralize business logic in hooks.
  - Use Next Image and SVGR icons.
  - Check existing patterns in `src/features/home/` before creating new structures.
- Don't
  - Don't inline `<svg>` or use `<img>`.
  - Don't hardcode routes, messages, or domain codes as strings; use constants files.
  - Don't fetch in components or spread business logic across components.
  - Don't add new patterns if an equivalent exists.
  - Don't create components without considering the atomic design level.
  - Don't mix feature-specific and shared components in the same directory.

### Conflict resolution
- If a rule conflicts with established code in the same area, follow the existing repository pattern for that area.

